{"id":4399,"date":"2021-12-06T15:21:22","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:21:22","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4399"},"modified":"2024-12-02T16:56:22","modified_gmt":"2024-12-03T00:56:22","slug":"2-3-return-functions-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-3-return-functions-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"2.3: Return Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa65091\" name=\"6a2213aa65091\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa651be\" name=\"6a2213aa651be\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain the difference between return and void functions<\/li>\n<li>Describe when return functions should be used in a program<\/li>\n<li>Describe when void functions should be used in a program<\/li>\n<li>Create a return function and use its output to influence the program<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Return Functions<\/li>\n<li>Final Code<\/li>\n<li>Return Functions with User Input<\/li>\n<li>Final Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge: Conversion Calculator<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa65269\" name=\"6a2213aa65269\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return Functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A <strong>return<\/strong> function is slightly different from the functions you have seen in the previous lesson. A return function returns a value or set of values. Let&#8217;s see how we can use return functions to create an inch to centimeter conversion calculator:<\/span><\/p>\n<p><iframe loading=\"lazy\" title=\"Return Functions\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" src=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/embed\/VsKBlSgIxPg?feature=oembed&#038;enablejsapi=1&#038;origin=https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" allowfullscreen><\/iframe><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now we know how to create a very basic calculator! In this activity, your CoDrone EDU will simulate a calculator. After adding two numbers together in the return function, it will throttle up and down for the value of the answer.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the time library, define a function called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">add<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0with <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">x<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> and<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> y<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> as parameters. Underneath, have the function return \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">x+y<\/code>.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create a variable called<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">that will store the return function. Next, include a <strong>print<\/strong> command that will display <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201cThe answer is\u201d<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> along with the answer.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">answer = add(2, 3)\r\n\r\nprint(\"The answer is \", answer)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your drone to take off, and then create a for loop with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0as its parameter. Within the loop, have the drone throttle up for half a second at 30% power, and then down for the same amount of time and power. Finally, have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nfor i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n \r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa65321\" name=\"6a2213aa65321\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y\r\n\r\n\r\nanswer = add(2, 3)\r\n\r\nprint(\"The answer is \", answer)\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nfor i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n    drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this out with multiple numbers! <em>What happens when your drone gets to bigger numbers?<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa653d7\" name=\"6a2213aa653d7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Return Functions with User Input<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In this step, you\u2019re going to take the calculator simulation even further by having a user input the numbers to add together!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Keep the first portion of your program the same:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set up a while loop so that while true the program will run. Inside the loop, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num1<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code>.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> In the input parameters, write <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201cInput first number (or q to quit): &#8220;. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Create another variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num2<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set its value to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">input()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">as well. Write <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u201cInput second number (or q to quit): &#8221; <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">in the parameters. Next, write an if statement so that if \u201cq\u201d is input, the program will break out of the loop if num1 or num2 is \u201cq\u201d.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">while True:\r\n\r\n    num1 = input(\"Input first number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n    num2 = input(\"Input second number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n    if num1 == \"q\" or num2 == \"q\":\r\n        break<\/pre>\n<p>While still in the while loop, create a variable called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">answer<\/code>\u00a0that will use the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">add(x,y)<\/code>\u00a0function to return the sum of <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num1<\/code><\/span> and <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">num2<\/code><\/span>. Next, include a <strong>print<\/strong> command that will print the answer.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">answer = add(int(num1), int(num2))\r\n\r\nprint(\"The answer is \", answer)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The rest of your program will be the same!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n    for i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa65432\" name=\"6a2213aa65432\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final program should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef add(x,y):\r\n\r\n    return x+y\r\n\r\n\r\nwhile True:\r\n\r\n    num1 = input(\"Input first number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n    num2 = input(\"Input second number (or q to quit): \")\r\n\r\n    if num1 == \"q\" or num2 == \"q\":\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 \r\n        break\r\n\u00a0\r\n    answer = add(int(num1), int(num2))\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"The answer is \", answer)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 for i in range(answer):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0    drone.set_throttle(30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.set_throttle(-30)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0  drone.move(0.5)\r\n\r\n    drone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa654b3\" name=\"6a2213aa654b3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: Conversion Calculator<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Many countries and systems use different ways of measuring quantities such as distance, mass, force, etc\u2026\u00a0 For this exercise, you will create a conversion calculator that takes in 4 different measurements and converts them to a different measurement. For example, we created a conversion function for inches to centimeters earlier in the lesson. You can choose any conversions you would like. To successfully complete this challenge, your program must:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Have a list that contains the abbreviations a user will choose from for the measurement to be converted<\/li>\n<li>Prompt for 2 input values, the amount, and measurement<\/li>\n<li>Have a function that takes in the two input values and returns the calculated value and measurement as a string<\/li>\n<li>Store the result as a variable<\/li>\n<li>Print the result in a sentence that tells the user what they input and what the result was (For example: \u201cYou entered 5 inches, which is 12.7 centimeters\u201d)<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>A few notes:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>You can add strings together by using the + sign\n<ul>\n<li>print(\u201cHello\u201d + \u201cWorld\u201d) will print \u201cHello World\u201d<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>You can do the same for variables and strings:\n<ul>\n<li>name = John<\/li>\n<li>print(\u201cHello\u201d + name) will print \u201cHello John\u201d<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213aa65503\" name=\"6a2213aa65503\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about return functions. You learned how they differ from void functions, when you should use return functions in a program, and how their outputs can be used with your program.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-3-return-functions-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4732,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2131,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-4399","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-intermediate","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4399","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":24,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4399\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13321,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4399\/revisions\/13321"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4732"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4399"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4399"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4399"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}